<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Intrinsic diffusion geometry with Riemannian metric matching.<span id="S0.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
The neural network learns a Riemannian metric (ellipses, top left) by implicitly averaging multiple rank-1 contributions through the metric matching loss (inset, bottom left).
This gives us access to the toolkit of Riemannian geometry methods via diffusion geometry.
Given a scalar field <math id="S0.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> (top middle), we can compute its intrinsic gradient <math id="S0.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the data geometry (bottom middle).
We estimate local data dimensions from the ratio of the metric eigenvalues (top right: red corresponds to higher dimension), and on-manifold interpolating paths between pairs of points (bottom right).
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although high-dimensional datasets are typical in machine learning, empirical evidence across domains suggests that the local intrinsic structure of many real-world datasets is effectively low-dimensional, commonly referred to as the manifold hypothesis.
The existence of this lower dimensional structure has led to the use of geometric or spatial tools on diverse data types.
These tools can probe intrinsic properties of data, including dimensionality, tangent spaces, and curvature, to name a few.
Such methods have been successfully applied in areas ranging from computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murase and Nayar, 1993</a>)</cite> and generative modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bamberger et al., 2026</a>)</cite> to single-cell data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moon et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and molecular dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Facco et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Diepeveen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Beyond applications, the manifold hypothesis plays an important theoretical role, offering insight into how deep neural networks generalize in high dimensions and mitigate the curse of dimensionality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pope et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kiani et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first step in a standard geometric data analysis pipeline is to build a graph whose nodes correspond to data points and whose edges encode local connectivity. The graph is either dense and weighted by a kernel function of the distance between the nodes, or sparse, such as <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-nearest neighbor (<math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN) graphs, possibly unweighted, with downstream analysis often sensitive to the hyper-parameter <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
While strong convergence guarantees hold in the infinite-data limit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coifman and Lafon, 2006</a>)</cite>, these approaches become computationally prohibitive at scale.
In particular, both computational and memory costs grow super-linearly, if not quadratically, with dataset size. Crucially, these costs persist at inference time, as processing new points require recomputing nearest neighbors for each query, preventing efficient out-of-sample extension.
These graph and kernel methods are also limited in their practical scope by their reliance on the pairwise distances between points, which cease to be discriminatory in high dimensions, and miss out on the many advantages of representation learning with neural networks that have made deep learning so successful <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">LeCun et al., 2015</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A more recent line of work leverages trained neural networks – such as VAEs, GANs, or diffusion models – to recover geometric information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stanczuk et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, often through the Jacobian of the trained network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arvanitidis et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. Since these networks are typically trained for representation learning or generative modeling rather than geometric estimation, existing approaches historically provide few theoretical guarantees that the recovered geometric quantities converge to the true underlying geometry in the infinite-data limit.
In the diffusion setting, however, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kharitenko et al. (2025)</a></cite> recently showed that the Jacobian of a Gaussian denoiser converges to the tangent-space projector, and used this for Riemannian optimization on data manifolds.
These approaches avoid pairwise distances and <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN graphs, but shift the cost to computing Jacobians of large networks at inference time, which can be expensive and numerically unstable in high dimensions.
Additionally, the geometry is extracted indirectly from a pretrained representation or generative model, rather than learned directly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we train a neural surrogate to regress the metric directly, rather than extracting geometry from neighborhood graphs or from derivatives of a pretrained models.
Our main contribution is a training objective inspired by the denoising objectives from diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
This loss allows a neural network to learn a Riemannian metric at every point by rewriting an intractable quantity as the marginalization of tractable ones.
Specifically, we learn an estimate for the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">carré du champ</span> operator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bakry et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, which can be used to recover the Riemannian geometry of the data via diffusion geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones, 2024a</a>)</cite>.
Given finite data, the minimizer of the loss corresponds to classical kernel-based geometric estimates, and in the infinite-data regime, the minimizer recovers the desired geometric quantities when the data distribution is locally supported on a manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate our method by applying intrinsic dimensionality estimation and tangent space recovery in high-dimensional settings where graph-based diffusion geometry becomes unreliable.
We also apply it to Riemannian optimization, where we compute intrinsic gradients to recover interpolating paths between data points that remain near data manifold.
By replacing neighborhood graphs with a learned, denoising-based surrogate, our approach makes diffusion-geometric analysis feasible in high-dimensional and large-scale settings where classical methods break down.
This allows the design of novel deep learning methods by directly inheriting tools from Riemannian geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones and Lanners, 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contributions:</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a neural surrogate for the carré du champ operator that enables amortized, dataset-size–independent inference of local geometric quantities without neighborhood graphs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a denoising-based, sample-wise <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">conditional Riemannian metric matching</span> objective that enables scalable training of the surrogate without explicit kernel construction or pairwise distance computations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We establish a theoretical connection between the denoising objective and classical diffusion maps, guaranteeing recovery of geometric quantities in the limit when the data is locally sampled from a manifold.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate empirically that metric matching recovers the correct geometry on known data manifolds, and leads to meaningful tangent vectors and high-quality on-manifold interpolating paths along high-dimensional image data.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Manifolds and Riemannian metrics.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional manifold <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a topological space that is locally
homeomorphic to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Riemannian metric</span> <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> assigns to each point <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>
an inner product <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{p}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{p}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> on the tangent space <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, varying
smoothly with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
The metric induces geometric notions such as lengths of curves, angles, volumes,
and the Laplace–Beltrami operator <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the case that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional
submanifold of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, the tangent space <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathbf{p}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathbf{p}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional linear
subspace of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> inherits a Riemannian metric from the ambient space by restricting the Euclidean inner
product <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle{\mathbf{u}},{\mathbf{v}}\rangle={\mathbf{u}}^{\top}{\mathbf{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle{\mathbf{u}},{\mathbf{v}}\rangle={\mathbf{u}}^{\top}{\mathbf{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> to vectors in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathbf{p}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathbf{p}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This choice of metric makes <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> an isometrically embedded submanifold of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, its
<em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">intrinsic gradient</em> <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f</annotation></semantics></math> is
a vector field on <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At each <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{p}}f\in T_{p}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{p}}f\in T_{p}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a tangent vector
pointing in the direction of steepest increase of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> along the manifold <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{p}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{p}}f</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the projection of the ambient Euclidean gradient (Jacobian)</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}}):=\Big(\tfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_{1}}({\mathbf{p}}),...,\tfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_{D}}({\mathbf{p}})\Big)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}}):=\Big(\tfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_{1}}({\mathbf{p}}),...,\tfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_{D}}({\mathbf{p}})\Big)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">at <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the tangent space <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathbf{p}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathbf{p}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion geometry and the carré du champ.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classical Riemannian geometry applies on manifolds, but real-world data rarely satisfy the stringent conditions of being a manifold (like having no branching points, and a uniformly constant local dimension).
Diffusion geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones, 2024a</a>)</cite> describes the geometry of more general spaces through the behavior of diffusion processes on them.
This applies to the low-dimensional but non-manifold data geometries that occur in practice, allowing the transfer of Riemannian geometry methods to generic data geometries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On a Riemannian manifold <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},g)</annotation></semantics></math>, the canonical diffusion is Brownian motion <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dx=dB_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dx=dB_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose evolution is characterized by its <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">infinitesimal generator</span>, the Laplace-Beltrami operator <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The diffusion is related to the Riemannian metric <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> by the carré du champ (CDC) identity</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\nabla f,\nabla h)=\frac{1}{2}\left(f\Delta_{g}h+h\Delta_{g}f-\Delta_{g}(fh)\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\nabla f,\nabla h)=\frac{1}{2}\left(f\Delta_{g}h+h\Delta_{g}f-\Delta_{g}(fh)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,h:{\mathcal{M}}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,h:{\mathcal{M}}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> are two smooth scalar fields.
In the theory of Markov diffusion operators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bakry et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, the infinitesimal generator <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> of any Markov process defines a bilinear form called the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">carré du champ operator</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\mathcal{L}}(f,h):=\frac{1}{2}(f\mathcal{L}h+h\mathcal{L}f-\mathcal{L}(fh))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\mathcal{L}}(f,h):=\frac{1}{2}(f\mathcal{L}h+h\mathcal{L}f-\mathcal{L}(fh))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">so that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\Delta_{g}}(f,h)=g\left(\nabla f,\nabla h\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\Delta_{g}}(f,h)=g\left(\nabla f,\nabla h\right)</annotation></semantics></math> on a manifold.
In diffusion geometry, this relationship is abstracted and used to define a generalized Riemannian geometry relative to an arbitrary Markov diffusion process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones, 2024a</a>)</cite>.
Specifically, the carré du champ operator of a general Markov process is used to <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">define</span> the Riemannian metric via <a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Diffusion geometry and the carré du champ. ‣ 2 Background ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 2</span></a>, from which we can construct the rest of Riemannian geometry.
This means that, by learning the carré du champ, we can access the toolkit of classical Riemannian geometry methods, such as intrinsic calculus, curvature, and topology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones, 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones, 2024b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Conditional metric matching</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we aim to learn the geometry of an underlying dataset by observing samples <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim p</annotation></semantics></math>, and estimating the carré du champ operator.
When the data lie on a manifold, a standard approach to approximate <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, uses the heat kernel</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\varepsilon}\left({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}\right)=\exp\left(-\frac{\|{\mathbf{x}}-{\mathbf{y}}\|^{2}}{2{\varepsilon}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\varepsilon}\left({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}\right)=\exp\left(-\frac{\|{\mathbf{x}}-{\mathbf{y}}\|^{2}}{2{\varepsilon}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with bandwidth <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This approach was originally introduced to justify kernel methods, but will also apply to metric matching.
We define the normalized diffusion operator</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{\varepsilon}f)({\mathbf{y}}):=\frac{(K_{\varepsilon}f)({\mathbf{y}})}{d_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{\varepsilon}f)({\mathbf{y}}):=\frac{(K_{\varepsilon}f)({\mathbf{y}})}{d_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(K_{\varepsilon}f)({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(K_{\varepsilon}f)({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\mathbb{E}_{X\sim p(x)}[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)f(X)]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\mathbb{E}_{X\sim p(x)}[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)f(X)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\mathbb{E}_{X\sim p(x)}[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\mathbb{E}_{X\sim p(x)}[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">are the kernel operator and a corresponding degree function, respectively.
Intuitively, <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{\varepsilon}f)({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{\varepsilon}f)({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a local average of <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> under a Gaussian window of scale <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on a manifold <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}f:=\Delta_{g}f-2g(\nabla\log p,\nabla f)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}f:=\Delta_{g}f-2g(\nabla\log p,\nabla f),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">the operator <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\epsilon}:=({\mathbf{I}}+P_{\varepsilon})/{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\epsilon}:=({\mathbf{I}}+P_{\varepsilon})/{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> converges pointwise to <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coifman and Lafon, 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Belkin and Niyogi, 2003</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A key property of the CDC operator is that it does not depend on the first-order drift terms, which cancel out when <a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 3 Conditional metric matching ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 4</span></a> is substituted into <a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Diffusion geometry and the carré du champ. ‣ 2 Background ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 3</span></a> by the Leibniz rule, so <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\mathcal{L}}=\Gamma_{\Delta_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\mathcal{L}}=\Gamma_{\Delta_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proofs ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix A</span></a>).
This means that the approximation <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\epsilon}:=\Gamma_{\mathcal{L}_{\epsilon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\epsilon}:=\Gamma_{\mathcal{L}_{\epsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math> converges pointwise to the Riemannian metric tensor <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\mathcal{L}}=\Gamma_{\Delta_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\mathcal{L}}=\Gamma_{\Delta_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can substitute <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\epsilon}=({\mathbf{I}}-P_{\varepsilon})/{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\epsilon}=({\mathbf{I}}-P_{\varepsilon})/{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> into <a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Diffusion geometry and the carré du champ. ‣ 2 Background ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 3</span></a> to rewrite <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> as the expectation</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\frac{\mathbb{E}_{X}\left[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)\bigl(f(X)-f({\mathbf{y}})\bigr)\bigl(h(X)-h({\mathbf{y}})\bigr)\right]}{2{\varepsilon}\,\mathbb{E}_{X}[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)]},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\frac{\mathbb{E}_{X}\left[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)\bigl(f(X)-f({\mathbf{y}})\bigr)\bigl(h(X)-h({\mathbf{y}})\bigr)\right]}{2{\varepsilon}\,\mathbb{E}_{X}[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)]},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which can be interpreted as the <span id="S3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uncentered</span> local covariance between <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> under the scale-<math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> diffusion generated by <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also consider the <span id="S3.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">centered</span> local covariance replacing <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{{\varepsilon}}f)({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{{\varepsilon}}f)({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{{\varepsilon}}h)({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{{\varepsilon}}h)({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\frac{\mathbb{E}_{X}\left[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)\bigl(f(X)-\left(P_{{\varepsilon}}f\right)({\mathbf{y}})\bigr)\bigl(h(X)-\left(P_{{\varepsilon}}h\right)({\mathbf{y}})\bigr)\right]}{2{\varepsilon}\,\mathbb{E}_{X}[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)]}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\frac{\mathbb{E}_{X}\left[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)\bigl(f(X)-\left(P_{{\varepsilon}}f\right)({\mathbf{y}})\bigr)\bigl(h(X)-\left(P_{{\varepsilon}}h\right)({\mathbf{y}})\bigr)\right]}{2{\varepsilon}\,\mathbb{E}_{X}[w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},X)]}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">These expectations can be estimated from samples using kernel-weighted neighborhood graphs, but this becomes computationally and memory intensive for many samples, and suffers from the curse of dimensionality in high dimensions.
In the next section, we show how to construct a scalable loss function and training procedure that allows a neural network to estimate <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)</annotation></semantics></math> from samples.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Conditional carré du champ matching</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to train a neural network to compute or approximate <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3 Conditional metric matching ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 5</span></a>.
A natural objective is to regress <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> under the smoothed density <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y}=p*\mathcal{N}(0,{\varepsilon}{\mathbf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y}=p*\mathcal{N}(0,{\varepsilon}{\mathbf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{marg}(\theta):=\EX_{Y}\Big[\big(\Gamma_{\varepsilon}^{\theta}(Y)-\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)(Y)\big)^{2}\Big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">𝔼</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{marg}(\theta):=\EX_{Y}\Big[\big(\Gamma_{\varepsilon}^{\theta}(Y)-\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)(Y)\big)^{2}\Big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">However, as we have seen, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3 Conditional metric matching ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 5</span></a>, which we will refer to as the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">marginal</span> CDC, is intractable.
We therefore introduce a tractable <span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">conditional</span> CDC, defined as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}):=\frac{1}{2{\varepsilon}}\left(f({\mathbf{x}})-f({\mathbf{y}})\right)\left(h({\mathbf{x}})-h({\mathbf{y}})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}):=\frac{1}{2{\varepsilon}}\left(f({\mathbf{x}})-f({\mathbf{y}})\right)\left(h({\mathbf{x}})-h({\mathbf{y}})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Since we chose <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y}=p*\mathcal{N}(0,{\varepsilon}{\mathbf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y}=p*\mathcal{N}(0,{\varepsilon}{\mathbf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{Y}({\mathbf{y}}|{\mathbf{x}})=(2\pi{\varepsilon})^{-D/2}w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{x}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y}({\mathbf{y}}|{\mathbf{x}})=(2\pi{\varepsilon})^{-D/2}w_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}})=(2\pi{\varepsilon})^{D/2}\mathbb{E}_{X}[p_{Y}({\mathbf{y}}|X)]=(2\pi{\varepsilon})^{D/2}p_{Y}({\mathbf{y}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{y}})=(2\pi{\varepsilon})^{D/2}\mathbb{E}_{X}[p_{Y}({\mathbf{y}}|X)]=(2\pi{\varepsilon})^{D/2}p_{Y}({\mathbf{y}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">so we can use Bayes’ rule to rewrite the marginal CDC as the following conditional expectation</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\EX_{X}\left[p_{Y}({\mathbf{y}}|X)\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)(X,{\mathbf{y}})\right]}{p_{Y}({\mathbf{y}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>𝔼</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\EX_{X}\left[p_{Y}({\mathbf{y}}|X)\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)(X,{\mathbf{y}})\right]}{p_{Y}({\mathbf{y}})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\EX_{X}\left[\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)(X,{\mathbf{y}})\mid Y={\mathbf{y}}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">𝔼</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\EX_{X}\left[\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)(X,{\mathbf{y}})\mid Y={\mathbf{y}}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">We now introduce the tractable conditional loss:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{cond}(\theta):=\EX_{X,Y|X}\Big[\big(\Gamma_{\varepsilon}^{\theta}(Y)-\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)(X,Y)\big)^{2}\Big]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">𝔼</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{cond}(\theta):=\EX_{X,Y|X}\Big[\big(\Gamma_{\varepsilon}^{\theta}(Y)-\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)(X,Y)\big)^{2}\Big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y|X\sim\mathcal{N}(X,{\varepsilon}{\mathbf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y|X\sim\mathcal{N}(X,{\varepsilon}{\mathbf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This loss can be sampled from, evaluated, and backpropagated through easily since <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from the data distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a noisy version of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and the conditional CDC is computed in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> since it does not involve an expectation or a normalizing constant.
The expectation decomposes over independent samples <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y|X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y|X</annotation></semantics></math>, so it trivially parallelizable and well suited for modern GPU-based mini-batch and distributed training.
Perhaps surprisingly, the conditional loss is equal to the marginal loss up to a constant independent of the parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, so the gradients of both losses are equal.
As such, optimizing <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{cond}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{cond}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to optimizing <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{marg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{marg}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{cond}(\theta)=\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{marg}(\theta)+C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{cond}(\theta)=\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{marg}(\theta)+C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{cond}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{marg}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{cond}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}^{CDC}_{marg}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Mean-centered carré du champ matching</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A similar argument applies in the the mean-centered case <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Conditional metric matching ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 6</span></a>.
Here, we additionally need to learn the terms <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\varepsilon}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\varepsilon}f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\varepsilon}h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\varepsilon}h</annotation></semantics></math> using other neural networks, which can be trained with the denoising loss <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EX_{X,Y|X}\left[\left((P_{\varepsilon}f)^{\theta}(Y)-f(X)\right)^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>𝔼</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EX_{X,Y|X}\left[\left((P_{\varepsilon}f)^{\theta}(Y)-f(X)\right)^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then freeze the parameters and regress the mean-centered CDC using <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2{\varepsilon}}\left(f({\mathbf{x}})-(P_{\varepsilon}f)^{\theta}({\mathbf{y}})\right)\left(f({\mathbf{x}})-(P_{\varepsilon}f)^{\theta}({\mathbf{y}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2{\varepsilon}}\left(f({\mathbf{x}})-(P_{\varepsilon}f)^{\theta}({\mathbf{y}})\right)\left(f({\mathbf{x}})-(P_{\varepsilon}f)^{\theta}({\mathbf{y}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> as the conditional CDC in <a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.1 Conditional carré du champ matching ‣ 3 Conditional metric matching ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Conditional Riemannian metric matching</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The loss in <a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.1 Conditional carré du champ matching ‣ 3 Conditional metric matching ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 10</span></a> can be applied to any pair of scalar fields <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, in practice we will train a network to regress the CDC <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(x_{i},x_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(x_{i},x_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> of each pair of coordinate functions <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,D</annotation></semantics></math>, and we show in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Recovering the metric from an embedded manifold ‣ 4 Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.​ 4.1</span></a> that the CDC <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)</annotation></semantics></math> of any pair <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,h:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,h:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> can be recovered from <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(x_{i},x_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(x_{i},x_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> by the chain rule.
Applying the conditional CDC loss jointly to all the coordinate functions <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> yields matrix-valued targets and predictions, and results in the aggregated Frobenius loss</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small\mathcal{L}^{\text{Riem}}_{cond}:=\EX_{X,Y|X}\Big[\big\|\Gamma_{\varepsilon}^{\theta}(Y)-\frac{1}{2{\varepsilon}}(X-Y)(X-Y)^{T}\big\|_{F}^{2}\Big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">Riem</mtext></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">𝔼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ε</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">Y</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ε</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">Y</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">Y</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">F</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\mathcal{L}^{\text{Riem}}_{cond}:=\EX_{X,Y|X}\Big[\big\|\Gamma_{\varepsilon}^{\theta}(Y)-\frac{1}{2{\varepsilon}}(X-Y)(X-Y)^{T}\big\|_{F}^{2}\Big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> outputs a positive semi-definite <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\times D</annotation></semantics></math> matrix approximating the CDC matrix.
When using the mean-centered loss, we note that the term <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{\varepsilon}Y)^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{\varepsilon}Y)^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes a standard denoising network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and He, 2025</a>)</cite>.
We condition the network on <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing it to capture the geometry of the data across different scales simultaneously. The full training algorithm can be found in <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix D Training Algorithms ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm.​ 2</span></a>.
This loss is most related to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al. (2021)</a></cite>, who propose a loss to estimate the second order score <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}\log p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}\log p</annotation></semantics></math> of the data. We discuss the exact relationship in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Extended comparison to higher order score matching. ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Positive semi-definite and low rank training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric and positive semidefinite (PSD) by definition, we first parameterize a neural network to produce a <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\times D</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> and make it symmetric PSD by setting <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)=M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)=M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> can be much larger than the intrinsic dimension, we also consider a low rank version where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> making <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> symmetric PSD with a rank upper bounded by the hyperparameter <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case we observe that the Frobenius norm in <a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 3.3 Conditional Riemannian metric matching ‣ 3 Conditional metric matching ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 11</span></a> can be simplified by expanding</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\big\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)-\tfrac{1}{2{\varepsilon}}(X-Y)(X-Y)^{T}\big\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\big\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)-\tfrac{1}{2{\varepsilon}}(X-Y)(X-Y)^{T}\big\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ \left\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)\right\|_{F}^{2}+\tfrac{1}{4{\varepsilon}^{2}}\left\|X-Y\right\|^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ \left\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)\right\|_{F}^{2}+\tfrac{1}{4{\varepsilon}^{2}}\left\|X-Y\right\|^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad-\tfrac{1}{{\varepsilon}}\left\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)(X-Y)\right\|^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ε</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad-\tfrac{1}{{\varepsilon}}\left\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)(X-Y)\right\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)\right\|_{F}^{2}=\left\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}\right\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)\right\|_{F}^{2}=\left\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}\right\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we can compute this loss without ever materializing any <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\times D</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, considerably improving the memory and time complexity.
The second term also does not depend on <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, so we form the simplified low-rank loss</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{LR}&amp;=\EX_{X,Y|X}\Big[\big\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}\big\|_{F}^{2}\Big]\\
&amp;\qquad-\frac{1}{{\varepsilon}}\EX_{X,Y|X}\Big[\big\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)(X-Y)\big\|^{2}\Big]\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">𝔼</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ε</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">𝔼</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{LR}&amp;=\EX_{X,Y|X}\Big[\big\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}\big\|_{F}^{2}\Big]\\
&amp;\qquad-\frac{1}{{\varepsilon}}\EX_{X,Y|X}\Big[\big\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)(X-Y)\big\|^{2}\Big]\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">If the manifold <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to take <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> to be at least <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, but cannot generally assume that <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=d</annotation></semantics></math> is enough to fully factorize the metric (this would require, at least, the condition that <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S3.SS4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">parallelizable<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1</span></span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">For example, the </span><math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-dimensional sphere embedded in </span><math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> dimensions is not parallelizable due to the hairy ball theorem, so </span><math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> is not sufficient in that case.</span></span></span></span></span>).
However, we prove in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Low-rank training is valid for 𝑟≥2⁢𝑑-1 ‣ 4 Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.​ 4.2</span></a> that picking <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2d-1</annotation></semantics></math> is always enough for the low-rank factorization to be valid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If a strict upper bound of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> to the intrinsic dimensionality is not desired, we consider the efficient parameterization <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)=M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)+\lambda{\mathbf{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)=M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)+\lambda{\mathbf{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a small Tikhonov regularization term ensuring strict positive definiteness. In this case, we get <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\lambda}_{LR}=\mathcal{L}_{LR}+2\lambda\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)\|^{2}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\lambda}_{LR}=\mathcal{L}_{LR}+2\lambda\|M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}(Y)\|^{2}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, see <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Low rank loss derivation ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix C</span></a> for a derivation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, the CDC <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a data-driven Riemannian metric which can be used to compute geometric objects via diffusion geometry.
In this section, we provide a theoretical guarantee for our construction.
Namely, when the data is locally sampled from a manifold and the loss is minimized, then <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to the true metric as <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Recovering the metric from an embedded manifold</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main theoretical guarantee is provided by the following theorem.
We note that the manifold assumption only needs to hold locally around a point <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the result extends to non-manifold data such as disjoint unions of manifolds or settings where the intrinsic dimension may vary across regions of the space. All proofs can be found in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proofs ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Convergence of the carré du champ)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> be a probability measure on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\text{supp}(\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\text{supp}(\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(x,\delta)\cap\text{supp}(\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(x,\delta)\cap\text{supp}(\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> is a manifold (of any dimension, possibly depending on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>), for some <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, with induced Riemannian metric <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> has a smooth density on this manifold.
Then
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)(x)\to g_{x}(\nabla f,\nabla h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}(f,h)(x)\to g_{x}(\nabla f,\nabla h)</annotation></semantics></math>
as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, for all smooth functions <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,h</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Carré du champ of the ambient coordinates converges to tangent space projection.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a special case, we consider the ambient coordinate functions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}({\mathbf{x}})=({\mathbf{x}})_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}({\mathbf{x}})=({\mathbf{x}})_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying the carré du champ to these functions yields, at each point <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the matrix</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\right)_{k\ell}\approx g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla x_{k},\nabla x_{\ell})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\right)_{k\ell}\approx g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla x_{k},\nabla x_{\ell}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the approximation becomes exact when <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let</p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G({\mathbf{p}})=(\Gamma(x_{k},x_{l})({\mathbf{p}}))_{k\ell}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G({\mathbf{p}})=(\Gamma(x_{k},x_{l})({\mathbf{p}}))_{k\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">be the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\times D</annotation></semantics></math> carré du champ matrix of the ambient coordinates at <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds to the pullback of the ambient Euclidean metric to the tangent space.
Because <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is embedded in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isometrically</span>, the eigenvalues of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G({\mathbf{p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G({\mathbf{p}})</annotation></semantics></math> are either 1 or 0, with eigenvectors pointing in the tangent and normal directions, respectively, and so <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G({\mathbf{p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G({\mathbf{p}})</annotation></semantics></math> the projection matrix <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}\to T_{\mathbf{p}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}\to T_{\mathbf{p}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We immediately obtain the following corollary by applying <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Convergence of the carré du champ). ‣ 4.1 Recovering the metric from an embedded manifold ‣ 4 Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 4.1</span></a> to the entries of the matrix <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})_{k\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})_{k\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the same assumptions as <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Convergence of the carré du champ). ‣ 4.1 Recovering the metric from an embedded manifold ‣ 4 Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 4.1</span></a>, the matrix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\right)_{k\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\right)_{k\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> converges (in every matrix norm) to the projection matrix onto the tangent space <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}\to T_{\mathbf{p}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}\to T_{\mathbf{p}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Carré du champ of the ambient coordinates is all you need.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The matrix <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})</annotation></semantics></math> of the CDC of the ambient coordinates is important because it suffices to evaluate the CDC of arbitrary functions.
If <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,h:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,h:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth, then</p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla h)=\sum_{k,l=1}^{D}\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_{j}}({\mathbf{p}})\frac{\partial h}{\partial x_{l}}({\mathbf{p}})g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla x_{k},\nabla x_{\ell})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla h)=\sum_{k,l=1}^{D}\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_{j}}({\mathbf{p}})\frac{\partial h}{\partial x_{l}}({\mathbf{p}})g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla x_{k},\nabla x_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">by the chain rule, so</p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(f,h)({\mathbf{p}})=\sum_{k,l=1}^{D}\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_{k}}({\mathbf{p}})\frac{\partial h}{\partial x_{l}}({\mathbf{p}})\Gamma(x_{k},x_{\ell})({\mathbf{p}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(f,h)({\mathbf{p}})=\sum_{k,l=1}^{D}\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_{k}}({\mathbf{p}})\frac{\partial h}{\partial x_{l}}({\mathbf{p}})\Gamma(x_{k},x_{\ell})({\mathbf{p}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">If we denote the Jacobians of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,h</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\partial}h({\mathbf{p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\partial}h({\mathbf{p}})</annotation></semantics></math>, as in <a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Manifolds and Riemannian metrics. ‣ 2 Background ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 1</span></a>, then this is just the matrix conjugation</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(f,h)({\mathbf{p}})=\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})^{T}\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}h({\mathbf{p}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(f,h)({\mathbf{p}})=\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})^{T}\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}h({\mathbf{p}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">We can formalize this in the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the same assumptions as <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Convergence of the carré du champ). ‣ 4.1 Recovering the metric from an embedded manifold ‣ 4 Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 4.1</span></a>, we have
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})^{T}\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}h({\mathbf{p}})\to g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})^{T}\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}h({\mathbf{p}})\to g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla h)</annotation></semantics></math>
as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, for all smooth <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,h</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Low-rank training is valid for <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2d-1</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Positive semi-definite and low rank training ‣ 3 Conditional metric matching ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.​ 3.4</span></a>, the low-rank factorization of the CDC can have topological obstructions if <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is too small.
We now prove that, for at least <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, the minimizer of the low-rank loss will still converge to the correct metric <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G({\mathbf{p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G({\mathbf{p}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Low-rank training is valid for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2d-1</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2d-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> minimises the low-rank loss (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ 3.4 Positive semi-definite and low rank training ‣ 3 Conditional metric matching ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 12</span></a>) then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\to G({\mathbf{p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})^{T}M^{\theta}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\to G({\mathbf{p}})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Intrinsic gradients</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The carré du champ gives us direct access to the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intrinsic</span> gradients <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f</annotation></semantics></math> of functions <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> defined on the data, with respect to the underlying geometry.
If <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is an (isometrically embedded) submanifold , we can represent <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f</annotation></semantics></math> in ambient coordinates by its <span id="S4.SS3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pushforward</span> from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At a each point <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, this is given by the vector</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla x_{1}),...,g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla x_{D}))\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla x_{1}),...,g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla x_{D}))\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the directional derivative of the function <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f</annotation></semantics></math> at the point <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By <a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Diffusion geometry and the carré du champ. ‣ 2 Background ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 2</span></a>, these terms are just the CDC <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla x_{i})=\Gamma(f,x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝐩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathbf{p}}(\nabla f,\nabla x_{i})=\Gamma(f,x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on the ambient space (such as a neural network or an ambient potential), we can apply <a href="#S4.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ Carré du champ of the ambient coordinates is all you need. ‣ 4.1 Recovering the metric from an embedded manifold ‣ 4 Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 13</span></a> and the fact that <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\partial}x_{i}({\mathbf{p}})={\mathbf{e}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\partial}x_{i}({\mathbf{p}})={\mathbf{e}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, to write</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E15X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Gamma(f,x_{i})({\mathbf{p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Gamma(f,x_{i})({\mathbf{p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\partial}x_{i}({\mathbf{p}})^{T}\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\partial}x_{i}({\mathbf{p}})^{T}\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E15Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})\right)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})\right)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">for each <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,...,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,...,D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Substituting <a href="#S4.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 4.3 Intrinsic gradients ‣ 4 Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 15</span></a> into <a href="#S4.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 4.3 Intrinsic gradients ‣ 4 Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 14</span></a>, we can compute the intrinsic gradient <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f</annotation></semantics></math> by the simple matrix-vector product
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})\in\mathbb{R}^{D}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})\in\mathbb{R}^{D}.</annotation></semantics></math>
We apply this method in the middle column of <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.​ 1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Riemannian optimization and interpolating paths</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One application of the Riemannian metric is to perform <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Riemannian optimization</span>, in which we aim to optimize a function <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> by gradient descent while remaining on the manifold <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can formulate this problem using the carré du champ (thereby also generalising it to non-manifold geometries), as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In general, when <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector field on an (isometrically embedded) submanifold <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:[0,1]\to\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:[0,1]\to\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a flow line (integral curve) of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has gradient
<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\gamma}(t)=X(\gamma(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\gamma}(t)=X(\gamma(t))</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\nabla f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\nabla f</annotation></semantics></math> is the gradient of some function <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(\gamma(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(\gamma(t))</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <a href="#S4.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 4.3 Intrinsic gradients ‣ 4 Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 14</span></a>,
so we can compute the flow along <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f</annotation></semantics></math> by solving the ODE</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\gamma}(t)=\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\gamma}(t)=\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})\boldsymbol{\partial}f({\mathbf{p}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We notice that, in the case that <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and <a href="#S4.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ 4.4 Riemannian optimization and interpolating paths ‣ 4 Convergence and the Riemannian toolkit ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 16</span></a> is discretized via an Euler method, then get</p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t+1)=\gamma(t)-\eta_{t}G(\gamma(t))^{-1}\boldsymbol{\partial}f(\gamma(t))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t+1)=\gamma(t)-\eta_{t}G(\gamma(t))^{-1}\boldsymbol{\partial}f(\gamma(t)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learning rate that may depend on <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G({\mathbf{p}})=\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G({\mathbf{p}})=\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the inverse of the carré du champ.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We apply this method to find interpolating paths between a source point <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and a target point <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> that stay close to the data manifold.
We aim to minimize the function <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f({\mathbf{x}})=\frac{1}{2}\|{\mathbf{x}}-{\mathbf{p}}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f({\mathbf{x}})=\frac{1}{2}\|{\mathbf{x}}-{\mathbf{p}}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with initial condition <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the interpolating path is the trajectory of <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When the Riemannian metric is the standard Euclidean metric, the resulting trajectory will just be the linear interpolation path in <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When the Riemannian metric is different, then these paths are not always guaranteed to converge to <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> or be the shortest paths possible.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14334v1/performance.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/239;" width="476" height="239"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of trained metric matching (mm) network against <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN-based baseline, on throughput (left) and performance (right). All experiments were run on a NVIDIA A10 (24GB) GPU.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply metric matching on synthetic and real-world datasets to evaluate i) its scalability to large datasets, ii) its accuracy in controlled settings with ground truth, and iii) its scalability to high dimensions.
First, we use synthetic datasets with known ground-truth geometry to assess the accuracy of intrinsic dimension and tangent space estimation, as well as the scalability of the method with increasing sample size.
Second, we apply our approach to a high dimensional image dataset to demonstrate that it can be trained and evaluated at scales where classical geometric methods based on <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-nearest neighbor graphs become impractical.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In each dataset, we estimate the intrinsic dimensionality and local tangent spaces.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones (2024b)</a></cite>, both quantities are derived from the predicted CDC matrix (usually computed via a <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN graph).
Intrinsic dimension is estimated from the eigenvalues of <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\varepsilon}({\mathbf{p}})</annotation></semantics></math>, while the tangent space (when <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is known) is given by the eigenvectors associated with its top <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues. We provide additional experimental details in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Experimental details ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Synthetic setting: scalability and accuracy</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with a controlled synthetic experiment on <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> points sampled uniformly from the <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional sphere embedded in <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. We vary the number of samples <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to evaluate the scalability of metric matching relative to baselines.
We also use the ground-truth tangent spaces to quantitatively evaluate tangent space prediction. Unless otherwise specified, we use <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=64</annotation></semantics></math>, and a fixed architecture across all dataset sizes: an MLP with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math>M parameters predicting a rank <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=16</annotation></semantics></math> matrix via low-rank training.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2606.14334v1/mnist_evecs.png" id="S5.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/249;" width="476" height="249" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of the first <math id="S5.F3.sf1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.F3.sf1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math>th, and <math id="S5.F3.sf1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>th eigenvectors of the CDC matrix learned by metric matching.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14334v1/agg_per_class_k100_cropped.svg" id="S5.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/288;" width="476" height="288"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Class-wise mean cumulative eigenvalues.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MNIST CDC eigen-analysis.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scalability.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first use this setup to evaluate the scalability of the neural surrogate for CDC-based geometric estimators against <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN-based CDC estimators, and report throughput (number of inferences per second) as a function of the number of samples. We compare both the raw CDC computation, and eigen-decomposition to identify the leading <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvectors, as is typical in tangent space prediction.
The raw neural CDC surrogate becomes advantageous over the raw CDC estimator at approximately <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>k points, and is over <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="82\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>82</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">82\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster for <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>M points and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="400\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>400</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">400\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster for <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>M points. In the small-data regime, the dominant computational bottleneck for tangent space prediction is eigendecomposition: low-rank training substantially reduces this cost by replacing a <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\times D</annotation></semantics></math> eigendecomposition with an <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times r</annotation></semantics></math> one, where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll D</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to speedups of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="67\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>67</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">67\times</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="132\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>132</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">132\times</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="194\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>194</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">194\times</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="359\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>359</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">359\times</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>k, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>M, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>M, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>M points, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Accuracy.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we evaluate the accuracy of tangent space prediction by measuring the Frobenius distance <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|UU^{T}-\hat{U}\hat{U}^{T}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|UU^{T}-\hat{U}\hat{U}^{T}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on a test set, where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a basis for the ground-truth tangent space and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is the estimator obtained by eigendecomposition.
The neural surrogate clearly outperforms <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN for datasets smaller than <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>M, with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="46\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>46</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">46\%</annotation></semantics></math> improved performance relative to the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN baseline, while closely matching it for larger datasets.
We note that the performance gains saturate for very large datasets, which we attribute to current architectural choices, which could be improved with further tuning.
We visualize the eigenvalues in <a href="#A6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix F Additional results ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.​ 7</span></a>, where the neural surrogate accurately captures <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> leading dimensions, while the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN CDC captures <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>.
These results indicate that the neural surrogate demonstrates better generalization than the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN–based CDC.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>High-dimensional image datasets</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classical kernel methods tend to be cursed by dimension, restricting their scalability to high-dimensional datasets.
We test metric matching in this setting by attempting to recover the intrinsic geometry of image datasets.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Geometric analysis of MNIST.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply metric matching to study the <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="784" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>784</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">784</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional image dataset MNIST, consisting of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math>k training and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>k validation images, which are black and white images representing digits <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>.
We train a standard UNet architecture with the low-rank metric matching objective <a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ 3.4 Positive semi-definite and low rank training ‣ 3 Conditional metric matching ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.​ 12</span></a> with rank <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>.
We visualize the quality of the learned geometry by plotting the eigenvectors of the CDC at validation images in <a href="#S5.F3.sf1" title="Figure 3(a) ‣ Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Synthetic setting: scalability and accuracy ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>.
We see that the first eigenvectors are highly interpretable, compared to the <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>th which appear much noisier.
In particular, the second eigenvector is close to a damped version of the original image, while the third and fourth are concentrated at edges and so generate translations.
We evaluate the importance of each tangent direction by its eigenvalue, and we plot the cumulative eigenvalue distribution (i.e. local explained variance) for each class in <a href="#S5.F3.sf2" title="Figure 3(b) ‣ Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Synthetic setting: scalability and accuracy ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a>.
We observe a sharp dropoff in eigenvalues, reflecting the low-dimensional structure.
The digit <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> has a faster drop, suggesting a lower intrinsic dimension, which is perhaps due to its relative visual simplicity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14334v1/inception_metric.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/114;" width="476" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Inception feature stability under tangent perturbation.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluating metric quality in high dimensions.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next apply metric matching to learn the carré du champ on three additional datasets: CIFAR-10 (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math>k colored <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> images, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=3072" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3072</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=3072</annotation></semantics></math>), celebA (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math>k colored <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=12288" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12288</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=12288</annotation></semantics></math>), and FFHQ (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70</annotation></semantics></math>k colored <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=196608" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>196608</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=196608</annotation></semantics></math>).
These examples lack a ground truth for evaluating and comparing models, so we introduce the following metric based on inception feature stability, inspired by the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Frechet Inception Distance</span> (FID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heusel et al., 2017</a>)</cite> for evaluating generative models.
We compute features from a standard pretrained inception network <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> used for FID, and consider how those features vary when the input image is perturbed.
If we interpret <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> as a Gaussian covariance matrix for the tangent space at <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and pick a target perturbation magnitude <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, we can sample <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\sim\mathcal{N}(x,\Gamma_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\sim\mathcal{N}(x,\Gamma_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, renormalize <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|v\|=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|v\|=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and compute the perturbation <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Phi(x+v)-\Phi(x)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Phi(x+v)-\Phi(x)\|</annotation></semantics></math>.
When the perturbation is tangent to the manifold, the difference in features should be small <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Srinivas et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, so we average the perturbation over <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to get a quality measure for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> at each magnitude <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, where no ground truth exists.
Normalization of the perturbation magnitude is important to avoid a bias towards low-rank metrics: if all the tangent vectors have the same magnitude, we measure the quality of <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">direction only</span>.
This evaluation is like the local version of the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">perceptual path length</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We plot the mean and standard deviation bars over the dataset, for each <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, as curves in <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Geometric analysis of MNIST. ‣ 5.2 High-dimensional image datasets ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.​ 4</span></a>.
As a baseline, we add perturbation in random directions, which corresponds to the Euclidean (isotropic) metric.
We also ablate metric matching against the score Jacobian metric <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kharitenko et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and Stein score metric <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azeglio and Di Bernardo, 2025</a>)</cite>.
The Stein score metric is a rough approximation given by a rank-1 correction to the ambient identity matrix, whose effect is pronounced in lower dimensions but vanishes as dimension increases (we see a slight improvement on randomness for MNIST and CIFAR but virtually no gain on CelebA and FFHQ).
The score Jacobian is also a principled estimator for the true geometry, and performs much better, but underperforms metric matching.
We expect that this is because its metric is full-rank, without the regularization effect of low-rank training of metric matching.
The results do not depend sensitively on bandwidth, and we report the best result for each method over a grid search.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also report the time and memory requirements of each method in <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Evaluating metric quality in high dimensions. ‣ 5.2 High-dimensional image datasets ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Riemannian Metric Matchingfor Scalable Geometric Modeling of Distributions" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.​ 1</span></a>.
An additional practical constraint is the high computational cost of computing the Jacobian, which requires <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim 3000\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mn>3000</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 3000\times</annotation></semantics></math> more memory and is <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim 62\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mn>62</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 62\times</annotation></semantics></math> slower per evaluation on CIFAR-10, and results in OOM on CelebA and FFHQ (despite 80GB GPU memory).
Conversely, the Stein score metric and metric matching have low-rank parametrizations so scale easily to high dimensions.</p>
</div>
